Explain CI/CD Flow in a Real Project

This is one of the most common questions asked in DevOps interviews.

A typical CI/CD pipeline automates the process of building, testing, and deploying applications from code commit to production.

🔹 Typical CI/CD Flow

1️⃣ Developer writes code and pushes it to Git (GitHub / GitLab / Bitbucket)

2️⃣ CI Tool triggers the pipeline
(Jenkins / GitHub Actions / GitLab CI)

3️⃣ Build Stage
The application is compiled and dependencies are installed.

4️⃣ Test Stage
Automated unit tests and code quality checks are executed.

5️⃣ Build Artifact
A Docker image or build artifact is created.

6️⃣ Artifact Storage
Images are pushed to a container registry (ECR / Docker Hub / Artifact Registry).

7️⃣ Deployment Stage
Deployment is done using Kubernetes / ECS / EC2 / VM servers.

8️⃣ Continuous Delivery / Deployment
Application is deployed to Dev → QA → UAT → Production environments.


🔹 Simple Real-World Flow

Developer → Git Push → CI Pipeline Trigger → Build → Test → Docker Image Build → Push to Registry → Deploy to Kubernetes


💡 Interview Tip:
In real projects, CI/CD pipelines are integrated with tools like Jenkins, Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform, and cloud services like AWS.
